## 💻 Command-Line Essentials
While modern operating systems rely heavily on Graphical User Interfaces (GUIs), early operating systems relied entirely on text-based command lines. Today, attackers rely heavily on the Command Line Interface (CLI) for scripting and automation—making it essential for defenders to master command syntax to analyze attacker behavior.

### 🔧 Core Commands
*   `hostname` – Displays the unique computer name. Essential for mapping out the local environment during an incident investigation.
*   `whoami` – Displays the current logged-in user account along with their respective privilege level and security context.
*   `ipconfig` – Displays standard TCP/IP network configuration details to verify network integrity and troubleshoot connection issues.
    *   `ipconfig /all` – Provides full, detailed syntax output including MAC addresses, DNS servers, and DHCP configurations. This helps defenders spot network spoofing or unauthorized configuration changes.
*   `/?` – The help switch. Appended to any command (e.g., `dir /?`) to display standard usage parameters and syntax documentation.
*   `cls` – Clears the command prompt screen to maintain a clean workspace during intensive investigations.

