## 🗂️ Registry Fundamentals
The **Windows Registry** serves as a centralized hierarchical database that stores configuration settings for the operating system, hardware devices, user preferences, and installed software applications. Because many malware strains modify registry keys to maintain access, defenders must monitor it closely.

*   **`regedit.exe` vs. `regedt32.exe`:** Historically, `regedit` was the standard 16-bit registry editor while `regedt32` was the 32-bit advanced version. In modern Windows operating systems, these utilities have been completely merged. Running either command will open the exact same modern GUI Registry Editor.
*   **Persistence Mechanisms:** Attackers routinely modify the registry to establish persistence, ensuring their malicious payloads execute automatically whenever the system boots or a user logs in.

